<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odyssey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Odyssey is an ancient Greek epic poem attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, probably composed in the late 8th or 7th century BCE. Its 24 books tell of the ten-year journey home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, and of his revenge on the suitors of his wife Penelope. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> it is one of the founding works of Western literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odyssey.jpg" alt="Odyssey" width="320" height="493" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zunkir · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fragment_Odyssee_2245_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek memory linked the city to its Bronze Age past: the Iliad names Knossos first among the Cretan cities led by Idomeneus, and the <b>Odyssey</b> calls it the great city where Minos ruled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Contact with the Greek world transformed Roman culture: Livius Andronicus translated the <b>Odyssey</b> into Latin around 240 BCE, Plautus and Terence adapted Greek comedy, Ennius wrote the epic Annales, and in the late Republic Cicero, Lucretius, Catullus, Sallust and Caesar created a classical Latin prose and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Iliad and the <b>Odyssey</b> of Homer, composed around the 8th or 7th century BCE, show the gods taking part in the Trojan War and the wanderings of Odysseus, intervening in human affairs, favouring their heroes and quarrelling on Olympus.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek literature begins with two of its greatest works, the Iliad and the <b>Odyssey</b>, attributed to Homer and composed around 700 BCE from a long tradition of oral poetry.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Livius Andronicus, a Greek from southern Italy, translated the <b>Odyssey</b> and staged the first Latin play adapted from a Greek original in 240 BCE; Plautus and Terence wrote comedies, and Ennius wrote an epic history of Rome in Greek metre.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer’s <b>Odyssey</b> calls Crete a land of ninety cities and names Knossos as the city where Minos ruled.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
